-- The last day of a period measured in years, on each reading (Articles 17(1)(a) and 20(5)). `add years` clamps 29 February to 28 February in a common year;
-- `Date` rolls it to 1 March; the third reading takes the day before the anniversary (inclusive counting: the day of the event is day one).
@ref Article 17(1)(a) (src:444) and Article 20(5) (src:528)
GIVEN reading IS A `How a number of years is counted from a day`
      start IS A DATE
      years IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a period of years` reading start years MEANS
    CONSIDER reading
    WHEN `the last day is the anniversary, and 29 February falls back to 28 February` THEN `add years` start years
    WHEN `the last day is the anniversary, and 29 February rolls to 1 March` THEN Date (DATE_DAY start) (DATE_MONTH start) (DATE_YEAR start PLUS years)
    WHEN `the last day is the day before the anniversary` THEN `the day before` (`add years` start years)

-- F6.22: is the last day named in a time limit (or in "until") inside the period? One helper for the permissions of Article 19 and the orders of Article 20.
@ref Article 19(9)(d), (e)(ii) and Article 20(2)(b) (src:503) (src:506) (src:523)
GIVEN reading IS A `Reading of the last day of a permission to act as a governor`
      day IS A DATE
      `the last day named` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the day is within a period whose last day is named` reading day `the last day named` MEANS
    CONSIDER reading
    WHEN `the day named is the last day of the permission` THEN day AT MOST `the last day named`
    WHEN `the permission ends before the day named` THEN day LESS THAN `the last day named`
